WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:05.249
Avengers: Infinity War is a nonstop ride of
block-rocking shockers, from the unexpected

00:00:05.249 --> 00:00:09.870
deaths of almost everyone fans love to the
crushing finale in which the villain clearly

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:12.380
wins, at least, until Avengers 4 comes out.

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:16.910
But one of the movie's most shocking moments
comes within minutes of the start of the adventure,

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:18.920
and we're not just talking about Loki croaking.

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:23.560
We're speaking, of course, of the thorough
beatdown that the Mad Titan Thanos administers

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:28.609
to the Avengers' Big Green Guy, who pops out
of nowhere on the Asgardian ship to ambush

00:00:28.609 --> 00:00:32.340
Thanos, with hopes of solving the problem
the villain presents before it even starts.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:36.370
What actually happens is that the Hulk gets
beaten down and more or less given the full

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:40.820
"Puny God" treatment after his attack on the
purple man with the really gross chin.

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:45.500
As a matter of fact, Hulk gets beat up so
hard during the fight that for the first time

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:48.760
ever, he's actually left unwilling to fight
on Bruce Banner's behalf.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:53.220
But how did the Mad Titan manage to hold his
own against the Hulk before acquiring enough

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:56.220
Infinity Stones to make him effectively unbeatable?

00:00:56.360 --> 00:01:00.820
Before the movie begins, Thanos gets the purple
Power Stone from Guardians of the Galaxy by

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:01.980
invading Xandar.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.400
Is that what he uses to get one over on the
Hulk?

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:05.580
The answer may surprise you.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:09.580
It turns out, when Thanos fights the Hulk
for the first time, he's not using the Power

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:10.400
Stone at all.

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:12.160
He's just that strong on his own.

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:16.859
According to the movie's commentary track
with directors Joe and Anthony Russo and writers

00:01:16.859 --> 00:01:21.810
Christopher Markus and Stephen McFeely, the
filmmakers behind Infinity War made a conscious

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:26.679
decision to leave the power-increasing Infinity
Stone out of the Thanos-Hulk fight.

00:01:26.679 --> 00:01:31.729
For the sake of maximum intimidation, Thanos
starts off the conflict at his base strength,

00:01:31.729 --> 00:01:34.640
a classic Dragon Ball sort of move if there
ever was one.

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:39.490
Joe Russo and Stephen McFeely have a little
back-and-forth on the film's commentary track

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:42.440
regarding Thanos' formidable baseline power
level.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.939
McFeely raises the issue during the Asgardian
ship scene, saying,

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:51.149
"Here you see the early establishment of just
how fearsome Thanos is even without [the stones],

00:01:51.149 --> 00:01:53.840
he's got one stone here, and I don't think
he's even using it."

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.220
To which Russo replies,

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:56.460
"He's not using it.

00:01:56.620 --> 00:02:01.060
This is to show that Thanos, the Genghis Khan
of the universe, is unbeatable in one-on-one

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:01.560
battle.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:03.540
He's conquered thousands of worlds.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:07.940
He's a much more polished fighter than the
Hulk, and he dismantles the Hulk fairly quickly."

00:02:08.149 --> 00:02:13.600
The screenwriters previously discussed Thanos'
awesome skill set in an interview with Collider,

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:17.180
saying they wanted "to announce Thanos as
the biggest villain in the MCU."

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:21.980
"By defeating hulk relatively savagely and easily you're hopefully."

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:26.640
there's a sense of dread over the course of the rest of the movie for anyone who will come up against him.

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:30.880
Anyone who's seen Infinity War would likely
attest to the fact that the early beatdown

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:35.020
Hulk takes from Thanos goes a long way toward
proving the villain's threat level.

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:38.720
Not only does he have the strength of the
Hulk, he also has a brilliantly ambitious

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:41.760
mind that runs circles around the Hulk's gray
matter.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:45.980
He's strong, which makes him threatening,
but it's his brain that makes him terrifying.

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:50.090
But even with the filmmakers' comments taken
into consideration, the answer as to why Thanos

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:54.340
proves to be more than an even match for the
Hulk comes down to something simple: Because

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:55.460
it's extremely cool.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:00.020
Make 19 movies' worth of a convincing comic
book universe on screen, and you've earned

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:04.480
the right to occasionally throw in something
awesomely unexpected like a Thanos who's way

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:06.320
stronger than anyone thought he'd be.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:10.060
No one expected Space Grimace to be able to
wipe the floor with Hulk like that.

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:13.280
Even on rewatch, it's a heck of a way to start
a movie.

